1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:01,838
The metal that hardens the steel in

2
00:00:01,649 --> 00:00:03,523
your tools was discovered twice.

3
00:00:03,652 --> 00:00:05,469
And the first time was in Mexico.

4
00:00:05,878 --> 00:00:07,500
The year is 1801.

5
00:00:07,628 --> 00:00:11,395
Andrés Manuel del Río, professor of mineralogy

6
00:00:11,206 --> 00:00:14,193
in Mexico City, analyzes a lead mineral…

7
00:00:14,395 --> 00:00:16,074
and finds a new element.

8
00:00:16,498 --> 00:00:18,349
He sends a sample to Paris.

9
00:00:18,478 --> 00:00:20,134
The French chemists reply:

10
00:00:20,152 --> 00:00:22,348
it's not new, it's a chromium mineral.

11
00:00:22,480 --> 00:00:24,595
They were wrong, but the discovery was

12
00:00:24,407 --> 00:00:25,318
set aside.

13
00:00:25,750 --> 00:00:28,553
Thirty years later, the Swede Sefström finds

14
00:00:28,365 --> 00:00:30,228
it again, in cast iron.

15
00:00:30,356 --> 00:00:32,024
He proves it is new and names

16
00:00:31,835 --> 00:00:32,207
it:

17
00:00:32,294 --> 00:00:35,028
vanadium, after Vanadis, a Norse goddess.

18
00:00:35,458 --> 00:00:37,321
That is why Del Río's name is

19
00:00:37,132 --> 00:00:38,949
nowhere on the periodic table.

20
00:00:39,078 --> 00:00:41,365
But the Royal Society of Chemistry puts

21
00:00:41,176 --> 00:00:41,950
it in writing:

22
00:00:41,968 --> 00:00:45,688
discovered in 1801 by Andrés Manuel del Río.

23
00:00:46,102 --> 00:00:47,953
Share it with someone who thinks science

24
00:00:47,902 --> 00:00:49,742
always gets it right the first time.

